\section{Ambiguity and context}{

Many names are not unique (~200 freguesia names are reused; the municipality
"Lagoa" exists in Faro and in Sao Miguel). Ambiguous names return \code{NA} with
a warning listing the candidates. To resolve them, either:
\itemize{
\item pass \code{context}: a parent unit name per element (municipio for
freguesias; distrito/ilha, NUTS region, or \code{"Acores"}/\code{"Madeira"} for
municipios), or
\item embed a qualifier in the name itself: \code{"Lagoa (Acores)"},
\code{"Lagoa (Faro)"}, INE-style \code{"Calheta (R.A.M.)"}, \code{"Lagoa [R.A. Acores]"}
and dash-separated forms like \code{"Calheta - RAA"} or \code{"Calheta-R.A.A"} are
all parsed automatically. A dash is only read as a separator when what
follows it is a recognized region — ordinary names containing dashes
(\code{"Albergaria-a-Velha"}) are never split.
}

A qualifier is only read as one when it is not already part of an official
name. Many freguesias end in a parenthetical of their own —
\code{"Bouro (Santa Maria)"}, \code{"Uniao das freguesias de Braga (Maximinos, Se e Cividade)"} — and those are matched whole, before any split is attempted.
}

\section{Spelling tolerance}{

Matching ignores case, accents, and punctuation, and expands \code{S.}/\code{Sta.}/
\code{Sto.}. If that still does not match, a second pass ignores Portuguese
particles, so official designations and their working short forms both
resolve: \code{"ULS da Guarda"} → \code{"ULS Guarda"},
\code{"ULS Trás-os-Montes Alto Douro"} → \code{"ULS Trás-os-Montes e Alto Douro"}.
Exact spellings always win; the report's \code{method} column shows \code{"loose"}
when this pass was needed.

A few units accept a second official spelling, so that data labelled either
way resolves: \code{"Vila da Praia da Vitoria"} for Praia da Vitória, and
\code{"Calheta"} for the Azorean Calheta (which \code{pt_registry} stores as
\code{"Calheta de Sao Jorge"}). Because Madeira also has a Calheta, a bare
\code{"Calheta"} is therefore \emph{ambiguous} and returns \code{NA} with a warning —
qualify it as \code{"Calheta (R.A.A.)"} or \code{"Calheta (Madeira)"}.
}

\section{Historic names}{

Administrative reforms rename and dissolve units, so names in older census and
administrative files often have no current counterpart. With
\code{historic = TRUE} (the default) names from \emph{every} vintage of the level are
matched, returning the code of the unit covering that territory in the
requested vintage; the report's \code{method} column shows \code{"historic"}. A name
found in the 2001 map is only used when no later vintage has it: most 2001
names were still in use in 2011, and for the few freguesias that lost
territory in between, the 2011 sense is the one that matches a single unit
today. This is
one mechanism, not a freguesia special case — it is also what makes
\code{"Minho-Lima"} resolve at \code{level = "nuts3"}, where the current name is
\code{"Alto Minho"}.

Live names win outright. A name still in use resolves to the unit bearing it
now, whatever it also meant in the past. Where a predecessor was later split
between several current units the name is genuinely ambiguous and returns
\code{NA} — pass a \code{context} or use codes. Historic names are matched exactly and
particle-loosely, never fuzzily.

A freguesia renamed while its map was in force keeps its earlier names as
names of that vintage, not as historic ones. The 2013 freguesias are named as
they were last, in CAOP 2024 and in INE's tables, and 25 of them were renamed
by law before that. \code{"Buarcos"}, the name of \code{060520} until 2015, therefore
matches it with \code{admin_vintage = "2013"} even when \code{historic = FALSE}.
}

\section{Vintages}{

The three axes are independent and each has its own argument. \code{admin_vintage}
chooses which units exist \emph{and which vintage's codes come back};
\code{nuts_vintage} and \code{health_vintage} choose the nomenclature that NUTS and
health columns are expressed in. See \code{\link[=pt_lookup]{pt_lookup()}}.

\code{admin_vintage} defaults to the current set, which is what you want for
joining old data onto today's map. To keep the data on its own vintage
instead — to map it against boundaries of the era — name that vintage:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{pt_match(c("Sé", "Maximinos"), level = "freguesia", context = "Braga")
#> [1] "030364" "030364"   # the union covering both today

pt_match(c("Sé", "Maximinos"), level = "freguesia", context = "Braga",
         admin_vintage = "2011")
#> [1] "030352" "030324"   # the units they were
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Codes are vintage-local.} None of the 302 freguesias restored in 2025
reused its pre-2013 code, even if it has the same name and same boundaries,
so a code is only accepted against the vintage it belongs to.
\code{pt_match("030882", level = "freguesia")} is \code{NA}, because that is
a 2013 code; with \code{admin_vintage = "2013"} it resolves.

\code{admin_vintage = "auto"} infers the vintage from the input and says which it
chose. This is the setting for INE data, which is published on the 2013
freguesia set even under the 2024 NUTS revision — so 134 units that were
disaggregated in 2025 are ambiguous against the current registry and exact
against their own.

INE prefixes a city's pre-2013 parishes with the city (\code{"Braga (Sé)"}) but
names the same kind of unit plainly elsewhere (\code{"Sé"} in Lisboa). All the
spellings resolve — \code{"Braga (Sé)"}, \code{"Sé (Braga)"}, and \code{"Sé"} with
\code{context = "Braga"} — because a bare name plus a context is recombined into
the parenthesised forms and retried, once the name as given has failed. A
trailing parenthetical never \emph{displaces} a \code{context} you passed: where it is
part of the official name rather than a qualifier, the context still applies.
}

\section{Fuzzy matching}{

With \code{fuzzy = TRUE} (requires the \code{stringdist} package), elements that
fail exact matching are compared against candidate names by optimal string
alignment distance. A fuzzy match is accepted only when the best candidate
is within \code{max_dist} and strictly better than the second-best candidate;
every accepted fuzzy match is reported via message. Fuzzy matching is
most reliable with a \code{context}, which shrinks the candidate pool.
}

